00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:35.010
With that, what are we
talking about today?

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.100
We will start with a brief
Cloud SQL introduction

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.010
and then talk about how to
scale it up and scale it out.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.880
From there, we'll get into a bit
more deeply technical content.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.130
And you'll see as we go
through the presentation today

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.500
that it gets progressively
more technical.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.140
We'll take a look back
to last year at Next.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.920
We showed performance numbers
last year for Cloud SQL.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.770
And we'll talk about how,
in the year that has passed,

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.570
we've been able to
increase Cloud SQL

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.315
performance by around double.

00:03:02.315 --> 00:03:04.690
We'll talk about the tuning
parameters that went into it.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.920
And then we'll talk a bit
more about our future work

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.590
here, which we're calling
16 kilobyte instance.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.020
It's a write
optimization enhancement

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.550
that's required the work
of many teams at Google.

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:17.670
And Ted will be taking
us through that.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.740
I think it's a really
interesting topic for us

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:23.430
to cover, is Cloud SQL, because
Cloud SQL, first and foremost,

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.730
makes sure that we
prioritize data integrity.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:30.240
And we're going to be turning
off, or at least talking

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.880
about turning off, as part of
this conversation, a feature

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.040
in MySQL called
double-write buffer.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.580
And when you search
on that term,

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:43.350
what you'll find is that
most online conversations end

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.140
with a statement
like, "don't turn

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.212
that off if you wish to
maintain data integrity."

00:03:48.212 --> 00:03:50.670
We're going to be talking about
doing that across our fleet

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.390
and for all
production instances.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:54.120
And I think it's going to be
interesting conversation for us

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.640
to have with you today, as
to why we think that's safe

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.223
and why we think it's the right
thing to do from a performance

00:03:59.223 --> 00:04:00.540
standpoint.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:01.350
Let's jump in.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.360
And let me first
start by pointing out

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.840
where Cloud SQL is in the
overall Google database

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:07.740
portfolio.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:09.240
Google has a fairly
broad portfolio,

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:10.460
as you can see on the slide.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:14.550
Looking rightmost, you see
third party or partner database

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:15.920
offerings on Google Cloud.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.145
Google Cloud generally has
a large partner ecosystem.

00:04:19.145 --> 00:04:21.269
The list you see on the
rightmost side of the cloud

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:24.810
is not comprehensive.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:26.347
There are many other--

00:04:26.347 --> 00:04:28.680
sorry, I'm having a little
trouble with the slides here.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.950
There are many other partner
offerings on Google Cloud.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:33.750
If you look leftmost
on the slide,

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.540
you see the in-memory databases
we just recently announced

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.037
in a managed Redis service.

00:04:38.037 --> 00:04:40.620
If you have caching needs, we
do have more offerings this year

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:41.536
than we did last year.

00:04:41.536 --> 00:04:44.550
And you can expect, generally,
the database portfolio

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.175
to continue to
increase over time.

00:04:46.175 --> 00:04:47.550
Looking just right
of center, you

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:49.590
see the relational
portion of the portfolio.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:52.170
It's made up of Cloud
Spanner and Cloud SQL.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:53.840
If you haven't checked
out Cloud Spanner

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.220
or been to one of the talks
at the conference this week,

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.720
I encourage you to
do so on Spanner.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.250
It's a great product
to get to know.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:04.920
Cloud SQL's role in the
portfolio is compatibility.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.850
Cloud SQL is meant to offer
very standard MySQL and Postgres

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:09.510
compatibility.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:13.350
We work hard to offer as close
to unmanaged MySQL and Postgres

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.230
as we can so that we can say,
if your application works

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:18.000
with MySQL or with
Postgres, it's

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.010
going to work with Cloud SQL.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:23.190
Most of our talk today
will focus on MySQL,

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:27.450
but we'll point out a few
Postgres items, as well.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:30.240
In addition to
compatibility, Cloud SQL

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.350
aims to reduce mundane
database administration that's

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:37.800
often associated with
running MySQL and Postgres.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.560
And as we talk
through this, I think

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:43.377
pointing out what "mundane"
means is important.

00:05:43.377 --> 00:05:45.210
When we talk about
things that are mundane--

00:05:48.624 --> 00:05:50.540
We may have to get a
little tech help up here.

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:52.040
Looks like we've
got slides that are

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:54.330
wanting to progress as
a mind of their own.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:58.440
When we talk about mundane,
we think about simple things

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:01.890
like telling Cloud SQL, watch
how much storage I'm using,

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:04.080
and as needed, increase
the amount of storage

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:05.760
so that I don't run out of it.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:07.560
We also talk about
some advanced concepts

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.674
that we'll get into as I
look at the overall stack.

00:06:10.674 --> 00:06:12.662
SPEAKER: [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:16.600
BRETT HESTERBERG: OK.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:18.020
We'll give it a shot here.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.372
So when you think
about Cloud SQL,

00:06:20.372 --> 00:06:22.580
Tim Kelton from Descartes
Labs does a pretty good job

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:23.288
of summing it up.

00:06:23.288 --> 00:06:26.270
He says Cloud SQL gives
his team more time

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:29.476
to work on products or
more time to work on things

00:06:29.476 --> 00:06:31.100
other than database
administration that

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:32.350
are valuable to his customers.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.280
And it's a good way to
think about Cloud SQL.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:38.150
When I think about Cloud SQL,
I tend to picture this stack.

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:42.110
This stack shows a
few different columns.

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:44.989
Leftmost--

00:06:44.989 --> 00:06:46.572
SPEAKER: Try just
using the space bar.

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:48.680
BRETT HESTERBERG: OK.

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:51.310
Leftmost is a stack
representing hardware.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:54.820
These are servers that you're
racking and stacking, worrying

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:58.094
about heating and
cooling, for example.

00:06:58.094 --> 00:07:00.510
In addition to this stack, as
you move right on the slide,

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.610
we start to think about
the operating system that

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:04.480
must be run on the
servers themselves

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.570
and keeping that operating
system up to date.

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:09.690
Moving further right, we finally
start to talk about databases.

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:11.500
This is the database
installation.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.610
It's software patching on the
database, keeping it secure,

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.800
and mandatory things
like making sure we

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.510
have backup scheduled so that
we're protecting our data.

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:20.970
Finally, rightmost
on the slide are

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:23.053
some of the more advanced
concepts in the database

00:07:23.053 --> 00:07:23.580
world--

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:27.330
setting up high availability
or replication for scale out,

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:29.400
making decisions about
when to failover,

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.950
when is a database unhealthy,
and whether we should do that

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:33.570
automatically or manually.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:35.840
All of this, of course,
gets tied together

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:37.380
or plugged together
with monitoring

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:40.350
so that we can understand
how our stack is performing.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:42.720
When I think of Cloud
SQL, I picture this stack.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.370
And I recognize
that Cloud SQL's job

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:48.060
is to turn this stack into a
single API call or a few UI

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:51.270
clicks so that if you're a
DevOps engineer or developer,

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:53.430
with a single API
call, you can spin up

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:56.400
a very simple development
database or a much more

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:59.760
complex production database
with high availability

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:02.010
and scale out for reads.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:05.280
What's not shown in this diagram
is one last important thing

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.390
about Cloud SQL.

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:12.000
And that is that it's backed by
Cloud SQL's 24-by-7 SRE team.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.130
Jason from RealMassive
points this out,

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:17.910
that because Cloud SQL is backed
by an SRE team whose job it

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.610
is to automate and monitor--

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:22.260
to keep the fleet
healthy, and then

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:24.570
when needed put hands
on keyboards to bring up

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.910
an unhealthy database--
his team worries less,

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:29.100
now, about databases
and carrying the pager,

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.990
so to speak, and more
about other things

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:32.190
in their technology stack.

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:34.890
Hopefully that gives you a
little feel for Cloud SQL.

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:36.700
Since this is a
performance talk,

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:38.909
let's get into
scaling Cloud SQL.

00:08:38.909 --> 00:08:41.429
I'm going to start with some
simple infrastructure concepts,

00:08:41.429 --> 00:08:44.340
and then we'll get into some
real detailed, especially

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:48.140
MySQL-level conversation.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:49.560
Scaling Cloud SQL up--

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:51.820
to start with, Cloud
SQL starts very small.

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:54.360
If you're new to Cloud
SQL, Cloud SQL instances

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:56.410
start at less than $10 a month.

00:08:56.410 --> 00:09:00.210
They scale up, right now, to
64 CPU cores and more than 400

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:01.770
gigabytes of RAM.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:03.867
These numbers change over time.

00:09:03.867 --> 00:09:06.450
When we present again next year,
these numbers will be larger.

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:07.616
They were smaller last year.

00:09:07.616 --> 00:09:10.560
So expect more cores, more
RAM, et cetera, over time.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:14.250
I think scaling up with
respect to CPUs and RAM

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.340
is pretty straightforward.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:17.940
What's a little
less intuitive is

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:20.790
how you scale storage
performance in Cloud SQL

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:23.666
and generally in Google Cloud.

00:09:23.666 --> 00:09:26.040
When you're thinking about
scaling performance of storage

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:31.200
in Google Cloud, note that you
scale by increasing capacity.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.100
And Cloud SQL, as you see in
the small screen shot here,

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:36.810
includes a
performance calculator

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:39.270
that shows how much storage
performance will be delivered

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:41.460
to your instance based
on a few factors--

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:45.240
how many CPU cores you
selected and the capacity

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:48.140
of storage you are
allocating to Cloud SQL.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:50.460
What I'll note here is that
as you increase capacity,

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:51.630
we deliver more IOPS.

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:53.537
And as you can see
by the calculator,

00:09:53.537 --> 00:09:55.620
we're only delivering about
half of the IOPS or so

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.620
that we could for this instance
based on the amount of capacity

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:00.060
that we've selected.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:02.400
Last thing I'll note is
that managing the mundane

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.220
that I talked about earlier,
where the simple check box here

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:07.680
tells Cloud SQL, watch
my storage capacity.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:10.110
If I'm getting too close
to filling up my disk,

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:11.910
grow it for me, and
do it on the fly.

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:13.545
Do it without taking
my database down.

00:10:13.545 --> 00:10:14.910
So I think it's a great
way to point out--

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:16.650
not only how to
scale up, but also

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:18.340
some of managing the mundane.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:18.840
OK.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.840
You've scaled up your Cloud SQL
instance, and at some point,

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.090
your workload is so
heavy that you're not

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:26.490
able to continue to burden
the single instance,

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:28.380
and it's time to scale out.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:31.980
Cloud SQL make scaling out easy
in that, again, with a few API

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:36.060
calls or UI clicks, you can add
replication and read replicas

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:38.610
so that in this
common configuration,

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:40.980
we can start to send
expensive read queries.

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:42.720
Maybe we're running
reports on Friday,

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.360
or we're using some
reporting tool.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:47.850
You can send those to a replica
rather than pointing those

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:50.349
at the primary instance, which
is already under write load.

00:10:50.349 --> 00:10:52.140
That way, the primary
instance can focus on

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:55.650
writes, and reads go
elsewhere, onto your replicas.

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:58.380
Just by show of hands
here, how many of you

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:01.290
are familiar with the
difference between MySQL's

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.990
semi-synchronous
replication and MySQL's

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:07.550
asynchronous replication?

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:08.510
A handful of people.

00:11:08.510 --> 00:11:09.926
A number didn't
raise their hands,

00:11:09.926 --> 00:11:12.130
so I'll give you the
one-minute overview.

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:15.220
Semi-synchronous replication
requires that a primary

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:19.060
instance and a standby
instance-- a replica--

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:23.102
both write a transaction
before that transaction

00:11:23.102 --> 00:11:24.560
is acknowledged to
the application.

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:26.770
So an application says,
"here's my transaction"

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:27.970
to the primary instance.

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:29.553
The primary instance,
when replicating

00:11:29.553 --> 00:11:31.480
using semi-synchronous
replication,

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:33.940
commits that transaction itself.

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:36.730
And it waits for the replica to
acknowledge that it's written

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:38.770
the transaction to a relay log.

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:41.920
The replica doesn't have to
actually commit the transaction

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:42.650
to data files.

00:11:42.650 --> 00:11:45.070
As long as it's
written to a relay log,

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:49.722
the replica will let the primary
instance know, OK, I've got it.

00:11:49.722 --> 00:11:52.180
And the primary instance then
comes back to the application

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:54.949
and says, OK, I'm ready
for your next transaction.

00:11:54.949 --> 00:11:56.990
This has, I think, two
important characteristics.

00:11:56.990 --> 00:12:00.250
One is that, in the
event of a failover,

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:02.950
we know that both our
primary and standby instance

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:04.190
have the data.

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:07.150
We're not going to lose
data on a failover.

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:09.040
And for that reason,
Cloud SQL employs it

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:12.460
as the only way we
replicate for a failover

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:14.080
replica, the only
way we replicate

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:15.744
for high availability.

00:12:15.744 --> 00:12:18.160
The second characteristic is
performance overhead-- right,

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:19.030
this is obvious.

00:12:19.030 --> 00:12:21.280
We're sending a write not
only to the primary instance

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:23.860
but also to this secondary
or standby instance.

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.560
And we're waiting for both
to say, "OK, I've got it"

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:29.320
before we let the application
write another transaction.

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:31.930
This differs from
asynchronous replication

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:34.390
in that in asynchronous
replication,

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:36.010
the primary instance
will happily

00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:39.100
continue to take transaction
after transaction

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:42.160
without waiting for the replica
to tell it, "OK, I've got it,"

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:42.670
as well.

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:44.680
In this way, you
can have a replica

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:47.530
that doesn't have the
very latest transactions

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:49.040
that the primary instance has.

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:51.700
Cloud SQL uses
asynchronous replication

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:53.440
only for read replicas.

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:55.450
These are your scale
out read replicas.

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:58.150
Again, for failover, we're
using semi-synchronous

00:12:58.150 --> 00:13:01.090
so we can guarantee we don't
lose data when we failover.

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:02.770
We also, with a
failover replica,

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:04.800
ensure that we put it
in a separate zone--

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:06.920
a separate failure
domain, if you will--

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:10.090
within a region so that we
have higher fault tolerance.

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:12.124
With a read replica
or for read scale out

00:13:12.124 --> 00:13:13.540
where we're using
asynchronous, we

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:15.040
let you put those
anywhere you want.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:17.770
And again, we don't
use them for failover.

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:20.470
So what you see in this diagram
is a very common configuration.

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:23.230
I have a high-availability
configuration.

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:24.760
I have semi-synchronous
replication

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:27.430
between my primary instance
and my failover replica.

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:29.570
And I've also added
an asynchronous read

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:30.730
replica for read scale out.

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:34.340
So I'm moving read load
off of my primary instance

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:36.009
so that it can
focus on write load.

00:13:36.009 --> 00:13:38.050
And this is what we're
talking about when we talk

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:39.910
about Cloud SQL scaling out.

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:43.150
Now, an important note here
is that while Cloud SQL makes

00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:45.100
all of this an
API call and makes

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:47.620
scaling up and
scaling out easy, it

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:50.140
doesn't tell you when to scale.

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:52.120
And that's important
because it still

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:55.450
requires you to know
your database numbers.

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:56.920
What you see in
this screenshot is

00:13:56.920 --> 00:13:59.650
Cloud SQL showing you
a basic set of metrics

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:01.540
in the cloud console.

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:04.840
In addition to those
metrics, we, in Cloud SQL,

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:07.450
enable the INNODB_METRICS table.

00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:09.370
I encourage you to get
to know that table.

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:11.500
It contains a wealth of
performance information

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:13.360
about MySQL and
will help you make

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:15.430
decisions about when
to scale up and when

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:17.710
to eventually scale out.

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:20.620
With that in mind,
let's take a look back.

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:26.530
And I'll ask Stanley to
take us back to Next 2017

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:29.790
and talk to us about
what's changed since then.

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:31.540
Let me, actually, take
that back from you.

00:14:31.540 --> 00:14:33.490
STANLEY FENG: Take back
the slide first, yeah.

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:34.930
BRETT HESTERBERG: If we have
trouble with the advancing,

00:14:34.930 --> 00:14:36.190
we'll move it back over here.

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:36.440
STANLEY FENG: OK.

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:37.380
SPEAKER: What it is is
we're getting interference

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:38.420
from another clicker somewhere.

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:40.260
So if we could stick
with the space bar.

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:41.640
BRETT HESTERBERG: OK.

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:42.590
STANLEY FENG: OK.

00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:45.274
BRETT HESTERBERG: I'll
advance your slides for you.

00:14:45.274 --> 00:14:46.440
STANLEY FENG: I can do that.

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:54.070
So let's take a look at
the Cloud SQL performance.

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:56.332
We do need the pointer, though.

00:14:56.332 --> 00:14:57.310
It's OK.

00:14:57.310 --> 00:15:01.010
I'll just wave my hands, then.

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:04.120
So let's take a look at the
Cloud SQL performance in 2017.

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:08.390
What's shown here is a
typical sysbench OLTP run.

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:11.290
What we run is a step
load kind of test,

00:15:11.290 --> 00:15:14.350
with each step doubling the
load of the previous step.

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:17.200
We started with one test
thread, and then two, then four,

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:19.890
then eight, and then
all the way to 512.

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:23.740
What's plotted here is TPS,
Transaction Per Second.

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:26.320
And each step runs
for about 20 minutes.

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:28.690
And for every second
of those 20 minutes,

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:32.030
we collect the TPS data
and then plot it here.

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:34.964
So this you're going to see a
number of these kind of graphs

00:15:34.964 --> 00:15:36.130
throughout the presentation.

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:39.040
This is our standard way of
benchmarking and plotting

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:39.970
results.

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:41.710
The benefit of this
is, you will be

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:45.160
able to see a lot of variability
throughout this 20-minute run,

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:49.040
because every second of the
data is displayed on this chart.

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:51.160
So what you see here
is, the red line

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:54.160
is our performance
in February 2017.

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:57.430
And the green line is the
performance in August 2017.

00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:00.190
And there are two main reasons
for these kind of improvements.

00:16:00.190 --> 00:16:02.980
One is, we did some
level of database,

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:06.070
and especially InnoDB
tuning, to stabilize

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:07.990
the performance of our
database and to allow

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:10.180
the underlying storage
performance improvement

00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:12.320
to show up as a database layer.

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:15.490
The second fundamental reason
for this kind of improvement

00:16:15.490 --> 00:16:17.800
is because Persistent
Disk has made

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:19.570
a lot of performance
improvements

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:21.820
in both latency and throughput.

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:25.810
Remember that Cloud SQL is built
on top of Google Cloud Platform

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:30.751
and uses Persistent Disk as an
underlying storage foundation.

00:16:30.751 --> 00:16:31.840
Next slide.

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:35.290
And you can see similar graph
for the Cloud SQL Postgres

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:36.070
performance.

00:16:36.070 --> 00:16:39.040
And this one is for Postgres HA.

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:41.440
And the blue line
is February 2017.

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:44.500
And the green line
is November 2017.

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:48.790
And for a similar reason, we
are seeing about 2x improvement

00:16:48.790 --> 00:16:51.130
in our end performance.

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:53.655
So then, you might
wonder, what about 2018

00:16:53.655 --> 00:16:57.670
and this data for 2017 one
year ago, and what about now?

00:16:57.670 --> 00:17:01.120
And actually, I encourage you
guys to find out by yourself.

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:03.820
Please feel free to take
pictures of this slide,

00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:06.099
because there is some
information here.

00:17:06.099 --> 00:17:08.170
So one tool I
really, really want

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:10.839
to bring up to your
attention is a tool

00:17:10.839 --> 00:17:13.050
called Perfkit Benchmarker.

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:15.839
So basically, we
decided to open-source

00:17:15.839 --> 00:17:18.114
all of our benchmarking
code-- not just

00:17:18.114 --> 00:17:22.599
database, but also compute,
storage, and everything--

00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:25.240
into a tool called
Perfkit Benchmarker.

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:27.040
And it's available on GitHub.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:29.020
For the database-related
one, it's

00:17:29.020 --> 00:17:31.630
called ManagerRelationalDB.py.

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:33.520
That would be the
starting point.

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:35.200
And the fundamental
reason we want

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:37.600
to open-source our
benchmarking tool

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:40.850
is to be purely open
and transparent.

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:44.290
So you get to see exactly
what we are benchmarking as.

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:45.340
You get to read the code.

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:47.717
You get to see how we
set up the framework.

00:17:47.717 --> 00:17:49.300
You get to see what
kind of parameters

00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:50.900
we're passing through the tools.

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:52.670
You get to see what
kind of tools we use.

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:55.690
And we actually cause into
sysbench and a pgbench

00:17:55.690 --> 00:17:57.740
when doing this kind
of benchmarking.

00:17:57.740 --> 00:18:01.430
So there's a benefit of
using Perfkit Benchmarkers.

00:18:01.430 --> 00:18:04.275
You can use exactly the same
tool and the same methodology

00:18:04.275 --> 00:18:08.080
on other providers and find
out the numbers by yourself

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:09.680
and make your judgment.

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:12.280
So our canonical
test configuration,

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:17.600
when you decide to try
the Perfkit Benchmarker,

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:19.990
our canonical test
configuration is basically,

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:22.870
we always try to make sure
the data set is large.

00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:24.640
So it's 5x RAM.

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:26.650
And we use n1-standard-16.

00:18:26.650 --> 00:18:29.140
And we always try
to fully provision

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:31.960
the underlying storage,
which it should

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:36.400
be 1.7 terabytes for PDSSD
and for the best performance

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:36.900
possible.

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:38.350
And then we place
the test client

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:40.060
in the same zone
as the database,

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:43.360
and we run each load step
for long enough so we

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:45.496
get to observe the variability.

00:18:45.496 --> 00:18:47.260
So next slide.

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:49.720
And then, besides the
benchmarking tools

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:54.880
and we open-sourced, we
also published our tuning

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:55.670
parameters.

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:58.770
And again, please feel
free to take pictures here.

00:18:58.770 --> 00:19:01.870
And there's a tinyurl
to the lower right

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:03.980
part of the presentation.

00:19:03.980 --> 00:19:06.770
And it shows a number
of InnoDB flags

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:10.610
retuned to achieve the
effect of the left-hand side.

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:13.450
As you can see, the performance
effect of the left-hand side

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:17.440
is more stable, more consistent
than the right-hand side.

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:21.340
And the documentation also
describes and explains

00:19:21.340 --> 00:19:23.950
the justifications
and reasons for why

00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:26.830
we tune each of those flags.

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:28.440
So next slide.

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:32.380
Now, looking ahead,
what is next?

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:36.130
So today, we're going to spend
quite a bit of our time in this

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:38.860
presentation talking about a
problem we want to tackle--

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:42.290
we have tried to tackle,
in the last few months--

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:43.990
or quarters, actually.

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:46.540
The problem is about
scaling writes at a very

00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:47.880
high concurrency load.

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:50.950
Now, high concurrency--
high is always

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:53.600
in the context of
some definition.

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:59.410
So here, we target sysbench
concurrency test thread at 512.

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:02.800
So what's shown here is
an eight-hour long run

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:07.090
of sysbench OLTP at 512 threads.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:09.010
As you can see, the
database performance

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:10.970
varies by at least 3x.

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:15.430
It can go as high as about
3,000, and drops down to 1,100

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:16.900
for the first half of the test.

00:20:16.900 --> 00:20:18.608
And then for the second
half of the test,

00:20:18.608 --> 00:20:21.190
it drops down to about
a couple of hundred--

00:20:21.190 --> 00:20:23.560
like 100 to 200--
from time to time.

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:27.850
So with this kind of
variability in database,

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:30.250
for many developers,
the database

00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:34.420
will be deemed essentially
useless because, first of all,

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:36.380
it's hard to the
capacity planning

00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:38.350
with this kind of performance.

00:20:38.350 --> 00:20:42.970
And second is that, for
those deep dips of TPS

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:45.460
during the second
half of the test,

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:48.640
it easily can translate into
timeouts from the application

00:20:48.640 --> 00:20:49.370
server.

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:51.130
So basically, a
performance problem

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:53.570
would turn into an
availability problem.

00:20:53.570 --> 00:20:57.760
And the third one is, if a
database is an important piece

00:20:57.760 --> 00:21:01.090
of a critical user journey or
user experience-- for example,

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:03.490
populating some of
the fields on the UI,

00:21:03.490 --> 00:21:06.190
or saving some
temporary states as user

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:09.610
going through a
set of bylog or UIs

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:11.710
to complete something
end-to-end--

00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:13.200
then this kind of
performance will

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:17.770
lead to pretty bad overall user
experience for the application.

00:21:17.770 --> 00:21:22.720
So let's take a look why
that problem happens.

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:24.790
Where, exactly, is the problem?

00:21:24.790 --> 00:21:30.040
So what's plotted here is
what we call InnoDB flush lag.

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:34.330
So for those of you who
have read into the InnoDB

00:21:34.330 --> 00:21:36.520
and the way the
InnoDB handles writes,

00:21:36.520 --> 00:21:41.000
basically, each writes,
when InnoDB process a write

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:45.440
and it writes the
transaction to the redo log.

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:48.610
And as soon as the transaction
is committed to the redo log,

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:51.400
the transaction will be
considered successful.

00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:55.752
Meanwhile, the data page in
memory will also be modified.

00:21:55.752 --> 00:21:57.835
But the datapicture will
be flushed asynchronously

00:21:57.835 --> 00:22:00.310
at the backend to the data file.

00:22:00.310 --> 00:22:03.070
Now, what's plotted here
is called a flush lag.

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:07.120
That is a difference in bytes
between the latest write that

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:10.030
has been successfully
committed to the redo log

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:14.200
minus the oldest write that is
in memory that still has not

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:16.720
been flushed to data file yet.

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:21.940
The flush log is generally equal
to the size of the redo log.

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:25.150
Now, in general, most of
the database-- or almost

00:22:25.150 --> 00:22:28.000
all database, especially
MySQL, has a limit

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:31.240
on the size of the redo log,
because if the redo log is

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:34.510
too large, it will affect
your crash recovery time.

00:22:34.510 --> 00:22:38.990
So when InnoDB sees a flush
log, or the size of the redo log

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:40.750
grows approaching the limit--

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:44.950
by default, it's at around
80% to 82% of that limit--

00:22:44.950 --> 00:22:48.840
then that is plotted as
a horizontal red line

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:49.930
on this chart.

00:22:49.930 --> 00:22:52.660
That is when the InnoDB
will go into what

00:22:52.660 --> 00:22:54.330
they call a sync flush state.

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:56.810
What a sync flush
state means, that

00:22:56.810 --> 00:22:59.900
is when the flush log
or the redo log size

00:22:59.900 --> 00:23:03.610
is at, in this
case, 820 megabytes.

00:23:03.610 --> 00:23:06.340
Then, when the next
notation comes in,

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:08.920
InnoDB will not
process a notation,

00:23:08.920 --> 00:23:12.400
but instead, it will go into
its in-memory dirty pages,

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:14.100
find out if the
oldest dirty pages

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:16.270
were flushed,
successfully flush,

00:23:16.270 --> 00:23:19.300
and writes that dirty
page into the data file

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:21.980
first, [INAUDIBLE] the
corresponding space

00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:22.770
in the redo log.

00:23:22.770 --> 00:23:25.330
Then, it will take
that new notation.

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:30.250
So this forms a stop-and-go
pattern in the end TPS graph,

00:23:30.250 --> 00:23:31.150
as we see.

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:33.070
And this is the
fundamental reason

00:23:33.070 --> 00:23:36.145
why you're seeing this kind
of performance variability.

00:23:36.145 --> 00:23:39.075
That, going to
the next slide, is

00:23:39.075 --> 00:23:42.490
we're going to see how we
can control the flush lag.

00:23:42.490 --> 00:23:45.070
So as you can see,
flush lag is how much

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:49.460
lag the dirty page flush is
behind incoming notation rate.

00:23:49.460 --> 00:23:51.740
So logically, it
seems very simple.

00:23:51.740 --> 00:23:53.500
If you want to
control flush lag,

00:23:53.500 --> 00:23:55.340
you just want to make
sure that database

00:23:55.340 --> 00:23:58.540
can flush dirty pages at
the rate that is higher

00:23:58.540 --> 00:24:00.220
than incoming write rate.

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:02.330
So there are two angles
to tackle this problem.

00:24:02.330 --> 00:24:04.750
One is, if you stop
incoming writes--

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:08.030
you have zero writes-- then you
will not have high flush lag.

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:09.170
But that's not acceptable.

00:24:09.170 --> 00:24:11.290
We want to make sure
that our instance can

00:24:11.290 --> 00:24:14.260
support as high an incoming
write rate as possible.

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:16.360
So the next angle
to attack this is

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:19.210
to make sure the database
can flush dirty pages as

00:24:19.210 --> 00:24:20.600
fast as possible.

00:24:20.600 --> 00:24:23.110
So there's a concept
called InnoDB doublewrite.

00:24:23.110 --> 00:24:24.930
Obviously, if you
disable doublewrite,

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:28.360
you basically cut the write
load during dirty page

00:24:28.360 --> 00:24:31.570
by half, essentially doubling
the rate the database can

00:24:31.570 --> 00:24:32.960
flush the dirty pages.

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:35.230
Now, we're going to talk
more about what is InnoDB

00:24:35.230 --> 00:24:37.370
doublewrite in the next slide.

00:24:37.370 --> 00:24:39.490
So for those of
you who have never

00:24:39.490 --> 00:24:42.800
heard of InnoDB doublewrite,
this is a quick refresh.

00:24:42.800 --> 00:24:45.510
It's basically,
InnoDB doublewrite

00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:47.900
does exactly what its
name says-- double

00:24:47.900 --> 00:24:49.410
write, write twice.

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:52.130
So when InnoDB wants
to flush a dirty page,

00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:55.630
it writes each dirty page
twice, sequentially--

00:24:55.630 --> 00:24:58.170
first to the
doublewrite buffer, then

00:24:58.170 --> 00:25:00.385
to the actual
place in data file.

00:25:00.385 --> 00:25:00.885
Then why?

00:25:00.885 --> 00:25:01.950
Why does it do that?

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:03.600
Why does it have to do that?

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:07.500
So it has to deal with a
concept called torn writes.

00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:12.240
A torn write is a
on-disk data page that

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:14.280
is partly new and partly old.

00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:18.330
It happens when the write
operation of a 16 KB data page

00:25:18.330 --> 00:25:22.300
was interrupted in the middle
of the write for any reason.

00:25:22.300 --> 00:25:24.180
Then, when the
torn write happens,

00:25:24.180 --> 00:25:26.730
then the only way to
recover from a torn write

00:25:26.730 --> 00:25:32.400
is to take that backup data
page from the doublewrite buffer

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:34.440
and use that to
recover torn write.

00:25:34.440 --> 00:25:38.040
You cannot use redo log
to recover torn page

00:25:38.040 --> 00:25:41.780
because to replay redo log,
you have to have the original,

00:25:41.780 --> 00:25:45.940
old page in its correct
form to replay the redo log.

00:25:45.940 --> 00:25:48.760
But with the torn page, you have
a page that is some part new,

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:52.800
some part old that you simply
cannot recover unless you have

00:25:52.800 --> 00:25:54.880
a backup copy of that page--

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:58.020
which is why InnoDB
has to do doublewrite.

00:25:58.020 --> 00:26:02.160
So then the next question go
a little bit step deeper--

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:04.669
how could a torn page,
torn write, happen?

00:26:04.669 --> 00:26:06.960
There are two major reasons
for a torn write to happen.

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:08.190
When there's a disk--

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:11.130
so any traditional
disks generally

00:26:11.130 --> 00:26:13.260
do not have atomic writes.

00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:17.050
And they can write at
512 byte sector size.

00:26:17.050 --> 00:26:19.780
And many disks have 4
KB physical block size.

00:26:19.780 --> 00:26:21.570
Even if they support
atomic writes,

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:23.790
it would be at 4 KB boundary.

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:27.740
And for a 16 KB data
page, that means

00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:31.185
that if it's anything that
fails during the 16 KB data

00:26:31.185 --> 00:26:34.470
page write, only part of that
might be written to the disk.

00:26:34.470 --> 00:26:35.520
That's the first reason.

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:37.270
Second reason is a file system.

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:40.050
Now, let's say you
have physical disks

00:26:40.050 --> 00:26:42.950
that you buy special hardware
that can support 16 KB

00:26:42.950 --> 00:26:44.190
atomic writes.

00:26:44.190 --> 00:26:46.560
The file system still
has to do some magic

00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:50.280
to make sure that the writes are
sent to disk at 16 KB aligned

00:26:50.280 --> 00:26:51.570
boundary.

00:26:51.570 --> 00:26:55.440
So in general, most of the
file system ext4 are those.

00:26:55.440 --> 00:26:58.110
They have 4 KB as
a data page size.

00:26:58.110 --> 00:27:01.350
And even the Linux kernel
has 4 KB as a page size.

00:27:01.350 --> 00:27:03.360
That means files
are only guaranteed

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:05.740
to align at 4 KB boundaries.

00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:08.970
Now, if a file that is aligned
only at 4 KB boundary, but it

00:27:08.970 --> 00:27:12.300
not aligned at 16 KB
boundary, then a 16 KB

00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:16.200
write into the file may actually
span two physical blocks

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:19.350
on disk, which is going
to break atomicity, again.

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:22.050
So-- next slide--

00:27:22.050 --> 00:27:24.750
the solution we come up
with to tackle this problem,

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:28.720
to allow us to safely
disable InnoDB doublewrite,

00:27:28.720 --> 00:27:32.760
is to try to achieve 16 KB
atomic writes in our storage

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:36.270
stack from top to the
bottom, end to end.

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:40.620
So first, I can introduce these
layers from bottom to the top,

00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:41.250
actually.

00:27:41.250 --> 00:27:44.220
First of all, we have
worked with very closely

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:48.960
with the Persistent Disk team
to create a 16 KB physical block

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:51.780
size PD that will provide
physical block level write

00:27:51.780 --> 00:27:52.960
atomicity.

00:27:52.960 --> 00:27:55.260
So I want to call out
this, because this

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:57.780
is very unique and
special in GCP,

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:01.290
that there are some prior
arts in the industry you have

00:28:01.290 --> 00:28:03.210
to purchase special hardware.

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:06.820
But with GCP, all you have
to do is to press up arrow

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:09.910
of your keyboard-- which
I suppose you have typed

00:28:09.910 --> 00:28:11.370
in GCloud before--

00:28:11.370 --> 00:28:13.980
and then add a new parameter
called physical block

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:15.530
size equal to 16 KB.

00:28:15.530 --> 00:28:16.570
Enter.

00:28:16.570 --> 00:28:20.400
You have a special disk that's
about 16 KB atomic writes.

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:23.520
So the next layer of the
stack that we worked on,

00:28:23.520 --> 00:28:26.280
which that, we'll talk
a lot more in detail,

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:31.490
is we configured and used a
new, innovative feature in ext4

00:28:31.490 --> 00:28:32.700
called BigAlloc.

00:28:32.700 --> 00:28:36.940
With BigAlloc, we forced file
system allocate and align files

00:28:36.940 --> 00:28:38.910
at 16 KB boundary.

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:42.780
And the third one is that to
ensure that the Linux kernel

00:28:42.780 --> 00:28:45.390
block layer file
system do not split

00:28:45.390 --> 00:28:48.180
writes into 16 KB
boundary, we worked

00:28:48.180 --> 00:28:50.370
with our
Container-Optimized OS team

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:53.100
to introduce extensive
regression and qualification

00:28:53.100 --> 00:28:56.640
test, and to ensure that when
upstream changes the kernel

00:28:56.640 --> 00:29:00.960
code, we catch those
invariants that were broken,

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:04.500
and we make sure that the
OS itself does not break.

00:29:04.500 --> 00:29:06.720
The fourth one that we
actually also worked

00:29:06.720 --> 00:29:09.210
with GC virtualization
teams to make

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:12.360
sure some of the critical
parameters in virtio layers

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:13.680
are configured properly.

00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:17.650
Mainly, it's max segment
size and maxed sectors KB.

00:29:17.650 --> 00:29:20.640
And there have to be
certain invariants

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:24.090
for the kernel to not split
writes into 16 KB boundary.

00:29:24.090 --> 00:29:26.100
The last one, at
the top level, is

00:29:26.100 --> 00:29:30.390
that with powers, we have to
config InnoDB to use O_DIRECT.

00:29:30.390 --> 00:29:33.870
So with that, I would like to
invite Ted onto stage to talk

00:29:33.870 --> 00:29:35.580
about container optimize--

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:36.820
oh, sorry.

00:29:36.820 --> 00:29:38.970
There's two more slides here.

00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:42.180
So I want to show the effect
of disabling doublewrites.

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:44.850
As you can see if you
recall the flush lag graph.

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:48.270
And previously, it was hitting
the red line consistently.

00:29:48.270 --> 00:29:50.300
This is with the 16 KB feature.

00:29:50.300 --> 00:29:53.160
As you can see, we are able
to effectively suppress

00:29:53.160 --> 00:29:55.740
a flush lag well
below the red line.

00:29:55.740 --> 00:30:00.450
And the next slide shows the
TPS result. Showing you here,

00:30:00.450 --> 00:30:03.540
on the middle of the
chart, is the TPS running

00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:07.070
exactly the same load--
eight hour and 512 threads.

00:30:07.070 --> 00:30:10.900
And the performance is generally
pretty stable and consistent.

00:30:10.900 --> 00:30:14.315
And the lower right corner was
doing 4 KB and doublewrite on.

00:30:14.315 --> 00:30:15.810
So sorry, Ted.

00:30:15.810 --> 00:30:16.429
Here.

00:30:16.429 --> 00:30:17.220
TED TSO: All right.

00:30:17.220 --> 00:30:21.010
So before I start--

00:30:21.010 --> 00:30:21.780
is my mic on?

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:22.740
Excellent.

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:25.380
So before we continue
the story of how

00:30:25.380 --> 00:30:29.100
we're able to enable the
16 K instance feature,

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:31.470
I want to take a brief
detour and talk a little bit

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:34.200
about Container-Optimized OS.

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:37.350
Some of you may have heard
about Container-Optimized OS,

00:30:37.350 --> 00:30:43.500
also known as COS, because it's
the preferred recommended image

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:45.990
for Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:30:45.990 --> 00:30:49.260
It's basically a
stripped-down Linux operating

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:52.830
system that has a
security-hardened kernel,

00:30:52.830 --> 00:30:54.810
verified boot.

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:57.660
And since it's stripped down,
it has just the software

00:30:57.660 --> 00:31:03.090
you need so we can minimize
its attack surface.

00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:06.270
Since it's based on the
open-source Chromium OS,

00:31:06.270 --> 00:31:08.340
we have a very
rapid release cycle.

00:31:08.340 --> 00:31:10.810
And that's important
for two reasons.

00:31:10.810 --> 00:31:14.730
The first is, we can
get security fixes out

00:31:14.730 --> 00:31:17.100
and into COS very rapidly.

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:18.870
And the second is,
it also allowed

00:31:18.870 --> 00:31:23.280
us to get new features,
new innovations deployed

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:24.960
very rapidly.

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:27.600
And not only is
it the recommended

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:30.330
image for Kubernetes
Engine, we also

00:31:30.330 --> 00:31:35.610
use it as the basis for
Cloud SQL's virtual machines.

00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:39.570
And so that's how we actually
deployed the kernel changes

00:31:39.570 --> 00:31:41.040
and some of the
user space changes

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:44.860
we needed to support
this particular feature.

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:49.140
So just to review, there
are two kernel guarantees

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:52.590
that we have to be
able to make in order

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:58.470
to make sure that 16 K database
pages don't get partially

00:31:58.470 --> 00:32:02.790
written to the disk in the
case of some kind of instance

00:32:02.790 --> 00:32:05.940
failure or hardware failure.

00:32:05.940 --> 00:32:09.720
And the first is that we need
to make sure that the database

00:32:09.720 --> 00:32:13.890
files are laid out in
logical chunks that

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:16.960
are 16 kilobytes--
what we call clusters.

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:19.594
And I'll talk more about
that on the next slide.

00:32:19.594 --> 00:32:21.510
And then the second thing
we need to make sure

00:32:21.510 --> 00:32:25.020
is that these I/O requests
don't get broken up

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:28.410
into multiple
requests, since that

00:32:28.410 --> 00:32:34.020
might lead to a torn write
in a failure scenario.

00:32:34.020 --> 00:32:36.960
So to accomplish
the first thing,

00:32:36.960 --> 00:32:41.490
we used a file system
feature called BigAlloc.

00:32:41.490 --> 00:32:45.750
It was originally developed
to optimize write patterns

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:51.450
for large-scale cluster file
systems such as Google's GFS.

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:55.620
And what it does is it
works around a restriction

00:32:55.620 --> 00:33:00.900
in the Linux virtual machine
which does not allow the file

00:33:00.900 --> 00:33:06.480
system block size to be larger
than the page size, which,

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:12.170
on Intel x86
architectures, is only 4 K.

00:33:12.170 --> 00:33:17.160
And so therefore, we can't make
the block size larger than 4 K.

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:19.110
So what we do instead
is we introduce

00:33:19.110 --> 00:33:21.960
a new concept, called clusters.

00:33:21.960 --> 00:33:25.200
And clusters are
composed of a power

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:29.040
of 2 number of 4 K blocks.

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:33.930
And data files get allocated
in units of clusters.

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:37.000
We still send the writes
out in 4 K blocks,

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:42.320
but the file system tracks
them in 16 K clusters.

00:33:42.320 --> 00:33:45.960
And this feature has been
around for quite a while.

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:49.920
It actually hit the mainline
Linux kernel in 2011.

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:54.300
But it only had been used for
very restricted use cases.

00:33:54.300 --> 00:33:56.790
As I said, the original
use case of this

00:33:56.790 --> 00:34:00.060
was for large-scale
cluster file systems.

00:34:00.060 --> 00:34:02.070
And so when we looked
at what we would

00:34:02.070 --> 00:34:06.450
need to do in order to
enable this feature for use

00:34:06.450 --> 00:34:09.690
in Cloud SQL, there
were two things

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:13.440
that we decided we had to do
to make it be production-ready.

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:17.750
The first is that we had to add
support for online resizing.

00:34:17.750 --> 00:34:22.170
This is so, as your database
grows and you have a need

00:34:22.170 --> 00:34:25.980
to store more data, Cloud
SQL will make a request

00:34:25.980 --> 00:34:28.560
to the persistent
disk to grow the size

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:29.699
of the persistent disk.

00:34:29.699 --> 00:34:31.469
And we need to be
able to grow the file

00:34:31.469 --> 00:34:35.909
system to use that extra space
for the expanded persistent

00:34:35.909 --> 00:34:36.842
disk.

00:34:36.842 --> 00:34:38.550
And then the second
thing we needed to do

00:34:38.550 --> 00:34:43.080
is, we had to add extensive
regression testing to make sure

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:48.540
that all of the new corner
cases that Cloud SQL might use

00:34:48.540 --> 00:34:51.480
were actually exercised
in our regression testing

00:34:51.480 --> 00:34:54.449
in the COS kernel to
make sure that there

00:34:54.449 --> 00:34:55.500
weren't any problems.

00:34:55.500 --> 00:34:57.960
Because even though
this BigAlloc feature

00:34:57.960 --> 00:35:00.330
has been around for
a long time, it's not

00:35:00.330 --> 00:35:02.430
a very commonly-used feature.

00:35:02.430 --> 00:35:06.120
And so there was the possibility
that there were corner cases

00:35:06.120 --> 00:35:08.280
that no one had
noticed, might not

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:11.590
have been working quite right.

00:35:11.590 --> 00:35:15.410
So for the second requirement
of what we needed to do,

00:35:15.410 --> 00:35:19.060
we had to audit the
Linux I/O stack.

00:35:19.060 --> 00:35:23.230
Historically, all operating
systems, including Linux,

00:35:23.230 --> 00:35:26.110
will merge adjacent
requests in order

00:35:26.110 --> 00:35:27.790
to make things more efficient.

00:35:27.790 --> 00:35:31.540
And then, if there are
limitations in either the SCSI

00:35:31.540 --> 00:35:34.090
controller or the
actual SCSI disk,

00:35:34.090 --> 00:35:37.540
it might break requests
in two in order

00:35:37.540 --> 00:35:40.480
to deal with those limitations.

00:35:40.480 --> 00:35:45.460
And we had to make sure that
each layer in the I/O stack--

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:47.590
starting at the
file system layer,

00:35:47.590 --> 00:35:51.220
to the Linux block layer,
to the Linux SCSI layer,

00:35:51.220 --> 00:35:55.150
to the virtio SCSI driver--

00:35:55.150 --> 00:36:01.130
all did not do what they're
allowed to do historically,

00:36:01.130 --> 00:36:04.960
which is to break requests up,
to make sure that if they did,

00:36:04.960 --> 00:36:08.310
they only broke them
at 16 K boundaries.

00:36:08.310 --> 00:36:11.020
And so that required
doing extensive audit

00:36:11.020 --> 00:36:13.990
of all of those code paths.

00:36:13.990 --> 00:36:17.840
And after we did that, we had
to ask ourselves the question,

00:36:17.840 --> 00:36:21.670
so what if future Linux
kernels might make changes

00:36:21.670 --> 00:36:23.710
in the storage stack
and all that auditing

00:36:23.710 --> 00:36:28.600
work might no longer be valid
for some new upstream kernel?

00:36:28.600 --> 00:36:32.200
And so what we did is we
added additional regression

00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:36.400
tests that actually tested
to make sure that writes

00:36:36.400 --> 00:36:39.560
would never get broken up
at inconvenient boundaries.

00:36:39.560 --> 00:36:42.850
And we added that to our
continuous testing regime

00:36:42.850 --> 00:36:46.750
that we use as part of our
build and release process

00:36:46.750 --> 00:36:49.630
for the kernels for
Container-Optimized OS.

00:36:49.630 --> 00:36:53.980
And that's to make sure that
this guarantee that we've

00:36:53.980 --> 00:36:55.720
made after auditing
the code will

00:36:55.720 --> 00:36:59.380
continue to be true in
future Linux kernels.

00:36:59.380 --> 00:37:02.980
And so the main thing, the
final point I want to make here,

00:37:02.980 --> 00:37:06.070
is that this effort
required collaboration

00:37:06.070 --> 00:37:09.520
across a large number
of teams at Google--

00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:13.930
the Cloud SQL team, the
Container-Optimized OS team,

00:37:13.930 --> 00:37:18.310
the Persistent Disk team, and
the GCE virtualization team.

00:37:18.310 --> 00:37:22.840
We all work together in order
to make this feature possible.

00:37:22.840 --> 00:37:25.390
And it's sort of a testimony
to the sort of things

00:37:25.390 --> 00:37:28.840
we can do when we take
really good people working

00:37:28.840 --> 00:37:31.750
at multiple layers
across the product

00:37:31.750 --> 00:37:35.850
to produce a feature like this.

00:37:35.850 --> 00:37:36.600
And back to Brett.

00:37:36.600 --> 00:37:38.730
BRETT HESTERBERG: That's
a great point, Ted.

00:37:38.730 --> 00:37:42.810
So to summarize a little bit
and talk about next steps,

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:45.660
we'll wrap up here
with just a few points.

00:37:45.660 --> 00:37:48.930
When you think about scaling
Cloud SQL, start simple.

00:37:48.930 --> 00:37:51.540
As I mentioned, scaling up is
intuitive-- except, perhaps,

00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:55.170
that scaling storage is aligned
with scaling its capacity.

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:58.260
So note that capacity and
performance have a relationship

00:37:58.260 --> 00:38:00.519
in Google Cloud generally,
and in Cloud SQL.

00:38:00.519 --> 00:38:02.310
Look forward, of course,
to larger instance

00:38:02.310 --> 00:38:05.220
types and continued storage
performance improvements.

00:38:05.220 --> 00:38:07.560
All the numbers on the slides
that we showed, hopefully.

00:38:07.560 --> 00:38:10.720
will be outdated in
some number of months.

00:38:10.720 --> 00:38:13.346
The commitment that Cloud
SQL has, in addition

00:38:13.346 --> 00:38:14.970
to being compatible--
which I mentioned

00:38:14.970 --> 00:38:17.940
is, your application just works
with Cloud SQL-- additionally,

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:20.530
we also are committed
to being open.

00:38:20.530 --> 00:38:22.560
And I think that shows
up in a few ways.

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:25.920
Stanley mentioned that
our Perfkit Benchmarker

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:27.120
is fully open-sourced.

00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:29.220
You see the tinyurl there.

00:38:29.220 --> 00:38:30.570
We host it on GitHub.

00:38:30.570 --> 00:38:32.174
We encourage you
to run it yourself.

00:38:32.174 --> 00:38:34.590
It should help you set up a
test environment, should allow

00:38:34.590 --> 00:38:37.590
you to compare Cloud SQL
against other database services,

00:38:37.590 --> 00:38:39.540
or a database that
you're running yourself.

00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:41.550
And it allows you,
because it's open source,

00:38:41.550 --> 00:38:44.370
to change the test parameters--
make them look more

00:38:44.370 --> 00:38:46.080
like your workloads.

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:47.820
The other thing that
Stanley pointed out

00:38:47.820 --> 00:38:50.647
is, when we tune a
database, we tune it

00:38:50.647 --> 00:38:52.230
not only for ourselves
but, hopefully,

00:38:52.230 --> 00:38:53.670
we tune it for everyone.

00:38:53.670 --> 00:38:56.381
And we've posted, for the past
couple of years, our tuning

00:38:56.381 --> 00:38:56.880
parameters.

00:38:56.880 --> 00:38:59.970
There's Cloud SQL,
on the third bullet

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:02.610
point you see on the slide,
which is the bottom URL.

00:39:02.610 --> 00:39:06.510
I encourage you, if you're
not running Cloud SQL on GCP,

00:39:06.510 --> 00:39:08.231
to start with our
tuning parameters,

00:39:08.231 --> 00:39:10.230
if you're going to run
MySQL of your own accord,

00:39:10.230 --> 00:39:12.360
maybe on a Compute
Engine VM, and then

00:39:12.360 --> 00:39:13.800
continue tuning from there.

00:39:13.800 --> 00:39:16.020
Our parameters will give
you better performance

00:39:16.020 --> 00:39:18.000
than a default
MySQL installation

00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:19.112
will on Compute Engine.

00:39:19.112 --> 00:39:20.820
And we think it's a
great starting point,

00:39:20.820 --> 00:39:22.650
if nothing else, for you.

00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:27.030
Ted talked about what it took
to align teams within Google so

00:39:27.030 --> 00:39:30.420
that we can safely turn
off doublewrite buffer

00:39:30.420 --> 00:39:34.140
and produce what we've
called 16 KB instances.

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:37.650
If you have interest in
seeing those for yourself

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:41.460
in Cloud SQL, please send an
email to this email address.

00:39:41.460 --> 00:39:43.740
We have started an
active alpha program.

00:39:43.740 --> 00:39:46.920
You're more than welcome to join
to see how write optimization

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:48.690
may benefit your workload.

00:39:48.690 --> 00:39:50.550
What you'll also see
from the Google teams

00:39:50.550 --> 00:39:53.700
is that we'll make these
options available to you

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:57.550
if you're choosing to run MySQL
outside of Cloud SQL, as well.

00:39:57.550 --> 00:39:59.670
So if you're using
Container-Optimized OS--

00:39:59.670 --> 00:40:02.310
and, as Stanley mentioned, you
can configure Persistent Disk

00:40:02.310 --> 00:40:04.500
with 16 KB block size--

00:40:04.500 --> 00:40:07.087
you'll be able to benefit
from this yourself

00:40:07.087 --> 00:40:09.170
as long as you stay within
some of the parameters.

00:40:09.170 --> 00:40:11.670
As you can see, there was a
lot of work from Linux kernel

00:40:11.670 --> 00:40:14.070
down through the storage stack
to make sure this is safe.

00:40:14.070 --> 00:40:15.653
But we're going to
do our best to make

00:40:15.653 --> 00:40:19.110
this open, like the rest of
Cloud SQL, to you, as well.

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:21.405
I want to point out a
couple presentation picks.

00:40:21.405 --> 00:40:23.280
We're on, really, kind
of the first major day

00:40:23.280 --> 00:40:24.350
of the conference.

00:40:24.350 --> 00:40:27.060
I'm going to give you two
shameless plugs to start out.

00:40:27.060 --> 00:40:30.600
First, we have a "Best Practices
for Migrating to Cloud SQL"

00:40:30.600 --> 00:40:32.400
session tomorrow afternoon.

00:40:32.400 --> 00:40:33.810
If you're interested
in migrating

00:40:33.810 --> 00:40:36.694
a workload to Cloud SQL,
stop by and take a look

00:40:36.694 --> 00:40:37.360
at this session.

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:39.690
It'll talk about how to do
that, especially to MySQL,

00:40:39.690 --> 00:40:41.250
with low downtime.

00:40:41.250 --> 00:40:44.070
Just later this afternoon,
we're doing an introduction

00:40:44.070 --> 00:40:45.600
to Cloud SQL and Kubernetes.

00:40:45.600 --> 00:40:47.580
If you'd like to learn
more about Kubernetes

00:40:47.580 --> 00:40:49.710
and how it can work
with Cloud SQL,

00:40:49.710 --> 00:40:52.170
take a look at this
session, which is just

00:40:52.170 --> 00:40:54.300
after today's session here.

00:40:54.300 --> 00:40:57.720
And last on the
list is IO225, which

00:40:57.720 --> 00:40:59.010
is "What's New with Spanner."

00:40:59.010 --> 00:41:00.760
If you haven't been
introduced to Spanner,

00:41:00.760 --> 00:41:03.067
I think this is a good
session to pick up what's new.

00:41:03.067 --> 00:41:05.400
And it will, of course, give
a bit of a product overview

00:41:05.400 --> 00:41:07.260
on Spanner, as well.

00:41:07.260 --> 00:41:09.710
With that, on behalf
of Stanley and Ted,

00:41:09.710 --> 00:41:12.480
I want to thank you for spending
the last 42 minutes with us

00:41:12.480 --> 00:41:15.850
and for bearing with a few
slight hiccups along the way.

00:41:15.850 --> 00:41:17.800
We'd love to open things
up to your questions.

00:41:17.800 --> 00:41:19.700
And if we run out of
time for questions,

00:41:19.700 --> 00:41:21.450
we'll ask that some
of our Cloud SQL folks

00:41:21.450 --> 00:41:22.825
stay toward the
front of the room

00:41:22.825 --> 00:41:25.030
so that you can catch up
with us after the session.

00:41:25.030 --> 00:41:27.050
Thank you, everyone.

